// plot-demo — a comprehensive showcase of the \use{plot} package.
//
// Render with:  texish-cli scripts/plot-demo.script
//
// The plot package is built entirely in the document language over the \picture
// vector-graphics layer. \plot sets up a data-to-device transform, draws the axes,
// tick marks, numeric labels, grid and title, then runs its body to draw the data
// series on top. This document exercises every series kind, every axis option, and
// the styling knobs the package exposes — each demonstrated under its own heading
// with a short note on what it does.

\use{document}
\use{plot}

\title{The \TeXish{} \mono{plot} Package}
\author{A comprehensive demonstration}
\maketitle

A \italic{plot} is a picture with a coordinate system on it: you declare the data ranges
and a few series, and the package maps data values onto the page, draws the axes and
labelled ticks, and strokes the series. Because every part of it — the prose, the
axes, the grid and the curves — is the same document language, a figure is just a box
that flows in the text like any other.

\section{Lines, scatters and legends}

A series is given an optional \italic{[colour]} and an optional \italic{[label]} before its
data, which is a flat \mono{x y x y …} list. A series with a label records a legend
entry; \verb|\legend| then draws the key in a corner of the data area
(\mono{ne}, \mono{nw}, \mono{se} or \mono{sw}). Here a \verb|\lineplot| strokes
a polyline through the points and a \verb|\scatter| drops a marker at a few of them.

\xrange{0}{6}
\yrange{0}{130}
\xlabel{time (s)}
\ylabel{height (m)}
\plottitle{Projectile}
\plot{
  \lineplot[royalblue][trajectory]{0 0  1 50  2 88  3 112  4 124  5 126  6 118}
  \scatter[crimson][samples]{1 50  3 112  5 126}
  \legend[se]
}

\section{Bars and reference lines}

The \verb|\bars| series draws a vertical bar from the axis to each value, centred on its
x position and a fraction \italic{plotbarfrac} of the x step wide. The \verb|\hline| and
\verb|\vline| commands draw a dashed reference line at a data value — a threshold, a
target, or as here the mean revenue.

\xrange{0}{7}
\yrange{0}{160}
\xlabel{quarter}
\ylabel{revenue}
\plottitle{Quarterly revenue}
\plot{
  \bars[teal]{1 95  2 110  3 80  4 140  5 120  6 150}
  \hline[crimson]{115.8}
  \vline[goldenrod]{4}
}

\section{Auto colours and marker shapes}

With an empty \italic{[colour]} bracket a series takes the next colour from the palette in
turn, so several series stay distinct with no manual colouring. The marker shape is set
with \italic{plotmarkshape} — \mono{circle}, \mono{square}, \mono{triangle} or
\mono{diamond} — and \italic{plotmarkr} sets the radius.

\xrange{0}{10}
\yrange{0}{10}
\plottitle{Four scatter series}
\set plotmarkr {3.2}
\plot{
  \set plotmarkshape {circle}   \scatter[][alpha]{1 2  3 5  5 4  7 8  9 6}
  \set plotmarkshape {square}   \scatter[][beta]{1 5  3 3  5 7  7 5  9 9}
  \set plotmarkshape {triangle} \scatter[][gamma]{1 8  3 7  5 9  7 6  9 4}
  \set plotmarkshape {diamond}  \scatter[][delta]{1 1  3 9  5 2  7 3  9 1}
  \legend[nw]
}
\set plotmarkr {2.6}

\section{Function plots}

The \verb|\fnplot| series samples a function of \italic{x} across the domain and strokes
the result. The expression is ordinary \verb|\calc| arithmetic written in terms of
\italic{x}, so any of the engine's math functions are available.

\xrange{-3}{3}
\yrange{0}{9}
\xlabel{$x$}
\ylabel{$y$}
\plottitle{$y = x^2$}
\plot{
  \fnplot[seagreen]{x*x}
}

When a range straddles zero the package draws a light axis line through it, so a curve
that crosses the origin shows where the sign changes. Several functions can share one
plot, and the tick step can be pinned with \verb|\xstep| / \verb|\ystep| instead of the
automatic \italic{nice} step.

\xrange{0}{6.283}
\yrange{-1.2}{1.2}
\xstep{1}
\ystep{0.5}
\xlabel{angle (rad)}
\plottitle{Sine and cosine}
\plot{
  \fnplot[][sin]{sin(x)}
  \fnplot[][cos]{cos(x)}
  \legend[se]
}

\section{Automatic ranging}

The \verb|\autorange| command scans a data list and chooses the ranges for you: x spans
the data exactly, y is padded by a small margin and pinned to a zero baseline when the
data is all non-negative. An explicit \verb|\xrange| / \verb|\yrange| afterwards still
overrides it.

\xlabel{day}
\ylabel{count}
\plottitle{Auto-ranged visits}
\autorange{1 12  2 19  3 15  4 27  5 22  6 31  7 28}
\plot{
  \lineplot[darkorange][visits]{1 12  2 19  3 15  4 27  5 22  6 31  7 28}
  \legend[se]
}

\section{Area and step series}

The \verb|\areaplot| series fills the band between a curve and the baseline, and
\verb|\stepplot| holds each value until the next x — a staircase for piecewise-constant
or cumulative data. The two compose in one plot.

\xrange{0}{8}
\yrange{0}{50}
\xlabel{day}
\plottitle{Cumulative total and daily rate}
\plot{
  \areaplot[lightsteelblue][total]{0 0  1 8  2 14  3 23  4 30  5 38  6 44  7 48  8 50}
  \stepplot[crimson][rate]{0 8  1 6  2 9  3 7  4 8  5 6  6 4  7 2}
  \legend[nw]
}

\section{Categorical axes and value labels}

\verb|\xcategories| names the x ticks instead of numbering them; the categories sit at
x = 1, 2, 3, … so bars line up beneath the names. Setting \italic{plotvalues} to 1 prints
each bar's value above it.

\yrange{0}{160}
\ylabel{revenue}
\plottitle{Revenue by quarter}
\xcategories{Q1 Q2 Q3 Q4}
\set plotvalues {1}
\plot{
  \bars[teal]{1 95  2 130  3 80  4 150}
}
\set plotvalues {0}

\section{Error bars and a trend line}

The \verb|\errorbars| series draws a capped whisker spanning $y \pm$ error at each point,
usually over a \verb|\scatter| of the same data. The \verb|\trendline| series fits the
least-squares line of best fit through the data and draws it dashed across the range.

\xrange{0}{10}
\yrange{0}{60}
\xlabel{dose}
\ylabel{response}
\plottitle{Measured response}
\plot{
  \scatter[steelblue][mean]{1 8  3 17  5 24  7 39  9 47}
  \errorbars[steelblue]{1 8 3  3 17 5  5 24 4  7 39 6  9 47 5}
  \trendline[firebrick][fit]{1 8  3 17  5 24  7 39  9 47}
  \legend[se]
}

\section{Minor ticks and tick-label formatting}

\italic{plotxminor} / \italic{plotyminor} subdivide each major interval with lighter minor
ticks and grid lines. \verb|\xtickformat| / \verb|\ytickformat| wrap every tick number
with a prefix and suffix — here a \verb|\$| before the cost values and a \verb|\%| after
the utilisation values.

\xrange{0}{100}
\yrange{0}{50}
\xlabel{utilisation}
\ylabel{cost}
\plottitle{Formatted axes with minor ticks}
\set plotxminor {2}
\set plotyminor {5}
\xtickformat{}{\%}
\ytickformat{\$}{}
\plot{
  \lineplot[seagreen]{0 5  20 9  40 16  60 25  80 38  100 47}
}
\set plotxminor {0}
\set plotyminor {0}
\xtickformat{}{}
\ytickformat{}{}

\section{Bubble charts}

The \verb|\bubble| series reads \italic{x y size} triples and draws a translucent disc at
each point whose \italic{area} (not radius) encodes the third value — the perceptually
honest scaling. Because the discs are translucent, two series overlaid in one plot stay
legible where they overlap.

\xrange{0}{10}
\yrange{0}{10}
\xlabel{price}
\ylabel{rating}
\plottitle{Products by market share}
\plot{
  \bubble[royalblue][premium]{2 8 12  4 7 30  6 9 8  8 6 22  3 5 16}
  \bubble[darkorange][budget]{3 4 18  5 3 9  7 5 25  9 2 14  6 6 28}
  \legend[sw]
}

\section{Styling the frame}

The frame is fully configurable. The grid can be turned off with \italic{plotgrid},
tick-label precision is set with \italic{plottickdec}, the bar width with \italic{plotbarfrac},
and the axis, grid and tick colours are all named variables. This plot turns the grid
off, widens the bars, and recolours the palette by overwriting its first entries before
the plot.

\global\mapset plotPalette {0} {mediumvioletred}
\global\mapset plotPalette {1} {darkslategray}
\set plotgrid {0}
\set plotbarfrac {0.8}
\set plottickdec {0}
\xrange{0}{6}
\yrange{0}{100}
\xlabel{station}
\ylabel{throughput}
\plottitle{Bare frame, wide bars}
\plot{
  \bars[][line A]{1 60  2 78  3 45  4 90  5 70}
  \legend[nw]
}
\set plotgrid {1}
\set plotbarfrac {0.6}
\set plottickdec {3}
\global\mapset plotPalette {0} {royalblue}
\global\mapset plotPalette {1} {crimson}

\section{Everything at once}

A final plot layers a filled area, a line with markers, a reference line and a fitted
trend into a single figure — the package's pieces are designed to compose, each reading
the same data format and sharing one transform.

\xrange{0}{12}
\yrange{0}{100}
\xlabel{month}
\ylabel{sales}
\plottitle{A year in review}
\set plotmarkshape {circle}
\plot{
  \areaplot[lightsteelblue][baseline]{0 20  3 28  6 35  9 48  12 60}
  \lineplot[royalblue][actual]{0 22  1 30  2 28  3 40  4 52  5 49  6 58  7 66  8 62  9 74  10 80  11 78  12 90}
  \scatter[crimson]{0 22  3 40  6 58  9 74  12 90}
  \trendline[seagreen][trend]{0 22  3 40  6 58  9 74  12 90}
  \hline[gray]{50}
  \legend[nw]
}

Each figure above is a self-contained box that flows in the running text, so a plot can
sit inline with prose, inside a figure float, or beside a table — all rendered through
the one document pipeline.
